From 078cbd6f0f50b13d5d51038904afb4887b780f42 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: abhay-byte <zenithblue.dev@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 08:45:33 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 7/7] panvk/csf: skip the preload drain when no fragment job
 wrote what the pass loads (PANVK_PRELOAD_BARRIER_TRACK)

A render pass that loads an attachment (a LOAD op, or the border of a
partial render area) records a barrier in cmd_init_render_state():
attachment writes -> sampled reads in the fragment shader, since the
preload reads through the texture unit.  On CSF that is a fragment
subqueue wait for all of its iterators plus a texture cache invalidate.
The wait covers every earlier fragment job, even when none of them
wrote the attachments the pass loads.  DXVK records a D3D11 frame as
many passes, most of them with LOAD (every render target rebind without
a clear, every split for a copy, a UAV barrier or a compute pass), so
consecutive passes on different targets cannot overlap their fragment
jobs.

Track, per command buffer, what the fragment jobs issued since the
fragment subqueue last waited for all of its iterators may have
written:

- issue_fragment_jobs() adds every plane of the pass's attachments,
  their multisampled-render-to-single-sampled images, their resolve
  targets and every framebuffer store and spill target (a direct MSAA
  resolve stores straight to the resolve image), as page-aligned
  address ranges, so aliased memory counts; up to 16 ranges, more or an
  unknown range (sparse, unbound, or a secondary that continues the
  primary's pass) makes the set unusable.
- Any barrier that makes the fragment subqueue wait for all of its
  iterators empties the set and makes it valid: every fragment job
  issued before it is done, those of earlier command buffers included.
- Until such a wait, or after secondaries ran (the graphics state is
  cleared then), the set is not valid.

When the set is valid and none of the loaded targets (in-bounds or
border loads: the view the preload reads, the attachment and its
preload view) overlaps it, the
preload barrier keeps its destination scope but has no source stages:
only the fragment-side texture cache invalidate is emitted, without
the wait.  A LOAD pass before the command buffer's first full fragment
wait still waits, so fragment jobs of earlier submissions are always
covered; the application's own barriers are unchanged.  JM is not
touched (there the barrier is a batch split).

Expected gain: none for a one-pass frame such as the DXVK cube; up to
one fragment drain (about 10-50 us each) per LOAD pass whose targets
were not rendered since the last drain, for multi-pass D3D11 frames.

Rationale: perf review 2026-10-26 C4 ("The preload barrier drains the
fragment subqueue at every pass that loads an attachment").  Knob:
PANVK_PRELOAD_BARRIER_TRACK=0 restores the unconditional 114 barrier; a
panvk_perf_knobs registry bit resolved once at the first
vkCreateInstance.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
